# SOS manual pages, built by tools/man-bundle.sh from man/*.txt; '@@ <topic>' starts a page.
@@ bootkey
NAME
    bootkey - would this firmware still start with secure boot on?

SYNOPSIS
    bootkey

DESCRIPTION
    Finds the public key the running firmware is signed with, hashes it as the
    boot ROM does, and compares that with the four boot key slots in the
    chip's one-time-programmable memory. It prints the firmware's key hash (or
    'not signed'), each slot that isn't blank with its mark ('valid', 'not
    marked valid', 'marked invalid'), whether secure boot is on, and a verdict:

        MATCH         a valid slot holds the hash of this firmware's key
        NOT YET       a slot holds it but isn't marked valid
        NO MATCH      no slot holds it
        NO VERDICT    the slots can't be read or the key can't be determined

    MATCH compares keys; it doesn't check the signature over the firmware,
    which 'picotool info -a' does. The command only reads. It exists for the
    procedure that secures a board: turning secure boot on with the wrong key
    hash in OTP leaves a board that runs nothing.

SEE ALSO
    version, rootsecret
@@ cat
NAME
    cat - print a file

SYNOPSIS
    cat <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the file to the terminal as it is. A job: Ctrl-C stops it, and a
    slow SSH client slows it down instead of losing output (about 130 KB/s
    over SSH). For reading a long file a screen at a time, with search, use
    'more'; it also keeps a file from sending the terminal a sequence, which
    'cat' doesn't.

SEE ALSO
    more, ls, echo
@@ cd
NAME
    cd - change the working directory

SYNOPSIS
    cd [<dir>]

DESCRIPTION
    Changes this shell's working directory, to '/' without an argument. Each
    shell (the console, every SSH session) has its own; paths given to the
    file commands are relative to it unless they start with '/'.

SEE ALSO
    pwd, ls
@@ chat
NAME
    chat - talk with the others in the chat room

SYNOPSIS
    chat

DESCRIPTION
    Joins the chat room: shows the last few lines and who is there, then each
    line typed at the 'chat> ' prompt goes to everyone else in the room, as

        [14:03 UTC] alice@laptop: hi

    Lines typed in the room are sent as they are: unlike a command line
    (QUOTING in 'man intro'), quotes, '|' and '>' in them are plain text.
    Joining and leaving are announced. In the room:

        /who          who is in the room
        /quit         leave (so do Ctrl-C, and Ctrl-D on an empty line)
        //text        send a line that begins with '/'

    Tab completes the word being typed: /who, /quit, or the name of someone in
    the room (a name with a space in it isn't offered).

    Messages are kept only in memory (the last 16), never in the log or on the
    card. See 'wall' for how messages arrive and what happens to a reader who
    falls behind.

SEE ALSO
    wall, who
@@ config
NAME
    config - show the saved configuration, or erase it

SYNOPSIS
    config
    config reset        (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    'config' shows what is saved: hostname, whether a console password is set,
    the Wi-Fi networks, the number of trusted keys, the NTS server, the
    periodic sync, and the RTC and chip temperature calibrations. Changes made
    by 'setup', 'wifi', 'key', 'hostname', 'time', 'temp cal' and 'passwd' are
    saved in flash immediately, survive reflashing, and only the console can
    make them.

    The record is stored sealed under a key derived from the board's root
    secret, in two alternating flash sectors with a sequence number and CRC, so
    a power cut while saving keeps the previous version. Its format only grows
    at the end: older firmware's records still load; firmware older than a
    record ignores it and starts empty.

    'config reset' erases everything, after you type 'yes': no Wi-Fi network,
    no trusted keys (so no SSH), no console password, hostname 'sos'. It also
    destroys the owner secret: the SSH host key becomes unreadable and a new
    one is made at the next setup (clients will warn), and SD cards this board
    encrypted can then only be read on a PC with the key shown when each was
    formatted; setting the board up again does not bring them back. The
    command says so before asking. A mounted encrypted card is unmounted. Set
    the board up again with 'setup', or provision it from the development
    machine (ninja provision).

SEE ALSO
    setup, wifi, key, hostname, time, passwd, rootsecret, sd
@@ cp
NAME
    cp - copy a file

SYNOPSIS
    cp <from> <to>

DESCRIPTION
    Copies a file. Copying to a directory keeps the name. A job: Ctrl-C stops
    it and removes the partial copy, as does the console locking itself. About
    580 KB/s on an unencrypted card, roughly half on an encrypted one. Files a
    running command has open can't be deleted or renamed meanwhile, and the
    card can't be unmounted or formatted. A long copy is synced to the card
    about once a second, so 'ls' shows how far it has got.

SEE ALSO
    mv, rm, ls
@@ crash
NAME
    crash - stop the firmware on purpose, to test the reset reporting

SYNOPSIS
    crash fault | stack | panic | hang      (console only)
    crash brownout                          (console only, test builds)

DESCRIPTION
    Testing only. Stops the firmware with a hard fault (a bad memory access),
    a stack overflow, an SDK panic, or a hang that the watchdog ends after
    SOS_WATCHDOG_MS (default 8 s). The next boot's log and 'uptime' report
    what happened, with the addresses to look up in build/sos.dis.

    'crash brownout', only in images built with SOS_TEST_COMMANDS, makes a real
    brownout reset: it raises the brownout detector's threshold above the core
    supply, and the reset puts it back. It clears what a power dip clears (the
    glitch detector's counts, the uptime before the reset). Refused while
    files are open, sd check or a format runs, or a Lua script runs.

SEE ALSO
    uptime, dmesg
@@ dmesg
NAME
    dmesg - the system log kept in memory

SYNOPSIS
    dmesg
    dmesg -f <facility>

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the last 4 KB of log messages, from any shell. With -f, only one
    facility's:

        kern     boot, resets, faults, the glitch detector, the root secret
        auth     console and SSH logins, passwords, every configuration change
        daemon   Wi-Fi, the SSH server, time, HTTPS, the SD card, the chat
        user     Lua scripts and 'logger'
        none     messages not tagged with one (there should be none)

    The console prints messages as they happen (at the 'console' level, see
    'log'); 'history' decides what is kept for dmesg, and every message kept
    is also written to the log files on the card, /var/log/<facility>.log
    (see 'log'). Messages are not printed
    while the console is locked or runs a full-screen program; they wait and
    appear afterwards, and are in dmesg meanwhile. Only printable ASCII is
    logged: other bytes in quoted text become spaces or '?'.

SEE ALSO
    log, logger, uptime
@@ echo
NAME
    echo - print text

SYNOPSIS
    echo <text>

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the text and a newline. The words are printed with single spaces
    between them; quote the text to keep its spaces or to print a '|', '>' or
    an apostrophe: echo "a   b", echo "it's" (QUOTING in 'man intro').

    '> <file>' after it is the shell's, as after any command: 'echo hi >
    /x.txt' writes the line to the file (replacing it), and '>> /x.txt'
    appends it. The same works for every command: 'dmesg > /d.txt'. The file
    is written even when the command prints nothing (it is then empty; 'echo
    > /x.txt' gives it one empty line). The command's own messages (its
    usage, its errors) go into the file too; only an unknown command is said
    on the screen, and then no file is made. Only the console may write
    under /certs and /var/log.

SEE ALSO
    cat, edit, tee
@@ edit
NAME
    edit - full-screen text editor

SYNOPSIS
    edit <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Opens the file (up to 128 KB) in a full-screen editor, on the console or
    over SSH; a file that doesn't exist is created on the first save. The text
    can grow by 32 KB while the file is open (save, quit and open it again for
    more), less while memory is short, and 'edit' says how much it can take.

    It needs a VT100/xterm-compatible terminal and uses its alternate screen,
    so the shell's output is back as it was afterwards. The screen size is
    asked from the terminal at the start and on Ctrl-L (so resize the window,
    then press Ctrl-L); without an answer it is 80x24. Up to 400 columns and
    200 rows of a bigger window are used.

KEYS
    typing, Enter, Tab          insert (UTF-8 text is fine)
    Backspace / Delete, Ctrl-D  delete before / at the cursor
    arrows, Home/End (Ctrl-A/Ctrl-E), PgUp/PgDn
                                move
    Ctrl-Left/Right (Alt-Left/Right, Alt-B/F)
                                a word back / forward (letters, digits, '_'
                                and any non-ASCII text are word characters)
    Ctrl-Home / Ctrl-End        start / end of the text
    Ctrl-S (Ctrl-O)             save
    Ctrl-Q (Ctrl-X)             quit; with unsaved changes, press it twice
    Ctrl-F (Ctrl-W)             find: type the text, Enter finds the next
                                match (empty: the last search again), Ctrl-C
                                cancels
    Ctrl-L                      redraw, and ask the terminal for its size

NOTES
    Saving writes <file>.~edit first, then replaces the file with it, so an
    interrupted save never leaves half a file; after a power cut 'edit <file>'
    picks the text up from the copy and says so. If two people edit the same
    file, the last save wins.

    Files with CRLF line ends keep them. Control characters, invalid UTF-8 and
    invisible or bidi characters show as '?' and are kept as they are. Wide
    characters take two columns and combining accents none; the cursor moves
    over a letter and its accents (up to two) as one. Long lines scroll
    sideways instead of wrapping.

    Ctrl-C doesn't stop the editor; Ctrl-Q does. If the SSH session drops, or
    the console locks itself, unsaved changes are lost. Log messages aren't
    printed on the console while the editor runs there: the status bar counts
    them and they appear when you quit. /certs can be opened from SSH but
    only saved from the console.

SEE ALSO
    more, cat, echo
@@ fetch
NAME
    fetch - download a file over HTTPS

SYNOPSIS
    fetch [-f] [-k] <url> [<file>]

DESCRIPTION
    Downloads <url> and saves the body exactly as received. Without <file>,
    the name is the last part of the URL's path, percent-decoded ('%20' is a
    space); a URL that ends in '/' or whose name can't be a file name on the
    card needs a <file>. That includes a name FatFs would store differently,
    such as one ending in a dot or a space: it is refused rather than
    changed, and giving <file> is the way round it. A directory as <file>
    saves the URL's name inside it.

    The body goes to <file>.part first and is renamed to <file> only once it
    is complete: the length the server announced arrived, or the last chunk,
    or the server ended TLS cleanly. A failure or Ctrl-C deletes the .part,
    so a cut download never looks finished. An existing <file> isn't replaced
    without -f; with -f it is moved aside to <file>.old until the download
    has taken its name, so a failed rename loses neither.

    Only a 2xx status saves anything. Any other status prints the status line
    and the first 256 bytes of what came with it, filtered as 'http get'
    filters text, and saves nothing.

    On the console a progress line (bytes, percent of the announced size,
    rate) is redrawn twice a second; over SSH or into a pipe only the summary
    is printed. The summary gives the size, time and rate, and the SHA-256 of
    what was written, computed as it was written, to compare with a
    published hash:

      sos> fetch https://example.org/pkg-1.2.tar.gz /apps
      -> https://cdn.example.org/pkg-1.2.tar.gz
      saved /apps/pkg-1.2.tar.gz: 183204 bytes in 2.4 s (74 KB/s)
      sha256: 9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08

    Redirects (301, 302, 303, 307, 308) are followed, at most 5, each a new
    connection with its own certificate check; each hop is printed as
    '-> <url>'. A redirect from https to plain http is refused; -k will allow
    it once plain http is supported (for now it is refused either way). A
    Location with a user name, or longer than a URL can be, is refused.

    URLs must start with https:// and the path must already be
    percent-encoded. The server's certificate must chain to one of the 2
    built-in roots (ISRG Root X1, which covers Let's Encrypt sites, and
    SSL.com's ECC root) or to one in the approved /certs/roots.pem. Many
    download hosts chain to other roots: the request then fails at the
    handshake, and fetch names the issuer it couldn't verify. Add that root
    to /certs/roots.pem and run 'tls roots approve' on the console ('man tls').

    Writes follow the same rules as 'cp': /certs and /var/log only from the
    console, the file is synced about once a second, and the card can't be
    unmounted meanwhile. One fetch runs at a time across all shells: another
    is refused with 'busy: fetch is running in another shell'. It counts as
    one of the two HTTPS requests that can run at once ('man http').

SEE ALSO
    http, tls, cp
@@ glitch
NAME
    glitch - the RP2350 glitch detector: setting, state and resets

SYNOPSIS
    glitch
    glitch on <0-3>        (console only)
    glitch watch <0-3>     (console only)
    glitch off             (console only)
    glitch default         (console only)
    glitch trig            (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    The chip's glitch detector resets it when its clock or core supply is
    disturbed, which is what a fault-injection attack does. 'glitch' shows its
    setting, whether it is armed now, how many times it has reset the board
    since power-on, and for the last such reset which detectors fired, how
    long the board had been up, and the chip temperature and supply voltage
    then (the last readings before the reset, taken every 10 s).

    'glitch on <0-3>' arms it by software, now and at every boot, at that
    sensitivity (0 lowest, 3 highest). 'on 3' is the default: a board is armed
    at the highest sensitivity unless set otherwise. This is not protection:
    it is armed a second or two into boot and software can disarm it; its
    purpose is to see whether the detector resets a healthy board before it is
    enabled for good in OTP. If it resets the board three times in a row
    within three minutes of starting, the next boot leaves it unarmed and says
    so. A detector reset loses the fault record.

    'glitch watch <0-3>' runs the circuits at that sensitivity without arming
    them: a trigger is logged, followed by a line with the chip temperature
    and supply voltage, and shown by 'glitch' ("armed, it would have reset the
    board"); nothing is reset. A
    detector that has triggered may stay triggered until the next reset, so
    each of the four is reported once per boot.

    'glitch off' disarms it, now and at boot: the way out if it resets a
    healthy board. 'glitch default' goes back to armed at 3. 'glitch trig'
    makes one detector fire, to check that a detector reset is seen and
    reported; it resets the board if the detector is armed.

SEE ALSO
    uptime, temp, power, version
@@ grep
NAME
    grep - only the lines of a command's output that hold a text

SYNOPSIS
    <command> | grep [-v] [-i] [-n] <text>

DESCRIPTION
    Passes on the lines of the command's output that hold the text, and drops
    the others ('dmesg | grep wifi', 'cat /log/sos.log | grep error | more').
    The text is a fixed string, not a pattern: no regular expressions and no
    wildcards. It is one word after the options, at most 64 bytes: quote a
    text with spaces ('grep "no route"' looks for "no route"; 'grep no route'
    prints the usage).

    -v    the lines without the text instead
    -i    upper and lower case alike (ASCII letters only)
    -n    each line starts with its number in the output and a ':'

    Options can be joined ('-vn'). A word starting with '-' that isn't made of
    these letters begins the text ('grep -x' looks for "-x").

    Lines are passed on whole, as soon as each one ends; a last line without
    a newline gets one. A line longer than 512 bytes is matched in pieces of
    512 bytes, each one on its own (and shown as a line of its own); with -n
    the pieces share the line's number.

NOTES
    grep works only after a '|': typed on its own it says so. A text that
    holds a '|' or '>' is quoted too ('grep "a|b"'). Error messages of the
    command go through grep like its other output. More about pipes in
    'man more'.

SEE ALSO
    head, tail, wc, more
@@ gunzip
NAME
    gunzip - decompress gzip files on the card

SYNOPSIS
    gunzip [-k] [-f] <file>...

DESCRIPTION
    Decompresses each file: 'x.gz' gives 'x' and 'x.tgz' gives 'x.tar'
    (the suffix in any case). Other names are refused ('unknown suffix'),
    as GNU gunzip does, and so is a name that is only the suffix ('.gz').
    The name and date stored in the gzip header are ignored: the output's
    name comes from the input's, and it is dated now.

    The output is written to '<name>.part' and renamed to '<name>' only when
    the whole input has been read and checked: each part's CRC-32 and length
    must match. Any error deletes the .part and keeps the input, so a
    'gunzip' that didn't finish leaves nothing half-written behind. The
    input is deleted after success unless -k is given. With several files,
    a file that fails is reported and the next one is decompressed.

    Files made by joining gzip files ('cat a.gz b.gz > c.gz' on a computer)
    decompress to the parts one after the other. Zero bytes after the end
    (padding from tape and block tools) are skipped with a line saying
    'ignoring trailing zeros'; any other bytes there are an error.

    A job: Ctrl-C stops it and removes the .part, as does the session
    ending. It writes 4 KB at a time, so a small file that decompresses to a
    lot only takes longer; a full card stops it with 'card full'. The .part
    is synced to the card about once a second, so 'ls' shows how far it has
    got.

OPTIONS
    -k    keep the input file
    -f    replace an existing output file (without it, gunzip refuses:
          'already exists'). The old file is moved aside to '<name>.old'
          until the output has taken its name, so a failed rename loses
          neither; if a '<name>.old' is already there, nothing is replaced
          and the output stays in the .part, as gunzip then says.
    Options come before the files, alone or together (-kf); '--' ends
    them, for a file whose name starts with '-'.

MEMORY
    About 50 KB of heap while it runs (zlib's 32 KB window and its state
    show as 'zlib' in 'mem'). It is refused when that would leave less than
    64 KB free, so a TLS handshake still fits. One of gunzip, zcat and gzip
    runs at a time across all shells; another is refused with 'busy: gunzip
    is running in another shell'.

SEE ALSO
    zcat, gzip, tar, cp, ls, mem
@@ gzip
NAME
    gzip - compress files on the card

SYNOPSIS
    gzip [-k] [-f] [-1..-9] <file>...

DESCRIPTION
    Compresses each file: 'x' gives 'x.gz'. A name already ending in '.gz'
    or '.tgz' (in any case) is refused ('already has a .gz suffix'), and so
    is a directory. The output is standard gzip that any gunzip reads, on
    the board or on a computer. Like 'gzip -n' on a computer, its header
    holds no file name and the date 0: gunzip names the output after the
    .gz file anyway.

    The output is written to 'x.gz.part' and renamed to 'x.gz' only when
    the whole input has been read. Any error deletes the .part and keeps
    the input. The input is deleted after success unless -k is given. With
    several files, a file that fails is reported and the next one is
    compressed. At the end it says the sizes, the time and the speed of
    the input: 'x.gz: <n> bytes from <n> (<percent>%) in <ms> ms (<n> KB/s)'.

    A job: Ctrl-C stops it and removes the .part, as does the session
    ending. A full card stops it with 'card full'. The .part is synced to
    the card about once a second, so 'ls' shows how far it has got.

    Memory decides the settings: a 4 KB window and zlib's memory level 6
    instead of the 32 KB window and level 8 a computer's gzip uses, which
    would take most of the heap. Files come out larger than with gzip on a
    computer: about 5% for logs, up to 20% for prose and source code. To
    save the most, compress there.

OPTIONS
    -k     keep the input file
    -f     replace an existing 'x.gz' (without it, gzip refuses: 'already
           exists'). The old file is moved aside to 'x.gz.old' until the
           output has taken its name, as with 'gunzip -f'.
    -1..-9 zlib's level: -1 is the fastest, -9 the smallest; 6 if none is
           given. The level changes the speed, not the memory.
    Options come before the files, alone or together (-k9); '--' ends
    them, for a file whose name starts with '-'.

MEMORY
    About 65 KB of heap while it runs (zlib's ~54 KB shows as 'zlib' in
    'mem'). It is refused when that would leave less than 64 KB free, so a
    TLS handshake still fits. One of gzip, gunzip and zcat runs at a time
    across all shells; another is refused with 'busy: gzip is running in
    another shell'.

SEE ALSO
    gunzip, zcat, mem
@@ head
NAME
    head - the first lines of a command's output, then stop it

SYNOPSIS
    <command> | head [<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    Passes on the first n lines of the command's output (10 without a
    number; 1 to 999999) and drops the rest. Once it has them the command is
    stopped at once, as Ctrl-C would stop it but without '^C': 'cat big.log |
    head 3' doesn't read the rest of the file. A command that isn't a job
    (dmesg, help) runs to its end, its further output dropped.

    Stages after it still get their turn: 'dmesg | head 5 | wc' counts the
    five lines.

NOTES
    head works only after a '|': typed on its own it says so. More about
    pipes in 'man more'.

SEE ALSO
    tail, grep, wc, more
@@ health
NAME
    health - self-checks of the board: the latest result of each

SYNOPSIS
    health
    health run [<name>]                 (console only)
    health full                         (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    SOS checks itself: at boot, then in the background while it runs. Each
    check reports PASS, WARN or FAIL with a short reason, or SKIP where it
    doesn't apply. 'health' shows the latest result of each check, in any
    shell: its name, status, when it was taken ('boot', '12 s ago', or 'never'
    for one that hasn't run) and the reason, then how long the boot checks
    took.

    The boot checks run before anything can make a key (in at most 100 ms):
        resets      WARN if the last reset was a fault, the watchdog, the
                    glitch detector, a brownout or a debugger (the text says
                    which; see 'uptime'), and on a boot loop: three resets in
                    a row, none asked for, each after under 3 minutes up (from
                    'uptime history')
        clocks      each running clock measured against its setting: WARN
                    beyond 1 %, FAIL beyond 5 %. They are measured against the
                    crystal, so this finds a PLL or divider set wrong, not a
                    crystal that drifts; the low-power oscillator's reading
                    (WARN outside 18-54 kHz) shows only a crystal about 2x off
        flash       the flash chip's JEDEC ID and size: FAIL if smaller than
                    the build expects, WARN on an unknown maker
        trng        raw bits from the random number generator's oscillator:
                    the repetition count and adaptive proportion tests of NIST
                    SP 800-90B, and the generator's own repetition test. FAIL
                    refuses making keys
        crypto      known answers for SHA-256, SHA-512, HMAC, AES-GCM, AES-XTS
                    and the Ed25519 public key, and that a changed GCM
                    ciphertext is refused; the text gives the time of each
                    group. FAIL refuses making keys and encrypting
    The background checks:
        crypto-ssh  30 s after boot, then daily: the known answers too slow for
                    boot, those of SSH: Ed25519 signing and verifying (and that
                    a changed message is refused), X25519. FAIL refuses as
                    crypto does
    and these read what SOS already keeps:
        glitch      every minute: WARN if the glitch detector's loop guard
                    left it off at this boot (see 'glitch')
        stack       every minute: WARN when core 0's stack peak is over 75 %
                    of its size, or core 1's (Lua) of its 32 KB; FAIL over 90 %
        heap        every minute: WARN under 64 KB free (back to PASS only
                    above 96 KB); FAIL under 16 KB (see 'mem')
        chip-temp   every minute: WARN over 70 C, FAIL over 85 C (the die's
                    temperature, see 'temp')
        supply      every minute: the lowest VSYS since the last run; on USB
                    power WARN under 4.3 V, and FAIL under 3.0 V (see 'power');
                    WARN if the brownout detector isn't on at 0.946 V
        core1       every 10 s while a Lua script runs: WARN when core 1 has
                    run no Lua for 30 s and isn't waiting for anything (a long
                    C call: Ctrl-C waits for it); FAIL when a stop was asked
                    and nothing has moved for 10 s (no new script until the
                    call returns, or a reboot)
        heap-sum    every 5 minutes: the heap's totals agree (what the
                    features hold is within what is in use; in use plus free
                    is the arena); FAIL if the heap reaches the stack guard
        rtc-drift   every 10 minutes: the drift the last sync measured (see
                    'time'). WARN when the RTC is surely more than 20 ppm off
                    (with the 'time cal' value to correct it), or the board's
                    crystal more than 65 ppm; FAIL beyond 1000 ppm. SKIP until
                    two syncs, and while the uncertainty is too large to say
    and these compare what is stored with what the board uses, hourly (the
    card every 6 hours), the first a minute or so after boot. They read
    only: none writes flash, OTP or the card, or repairs anything:
        config      both configuration records: PASS when the one in use is
                    sealed and the other is empty or an older sealed one;
                    WARN when the other is damaged (no fallback if a save
                    fails), or the one in use is still plain; FAIL when no
                    record can be used though one is there (see 'config')
        rootsecret  where the root secret comes from, against the OTP flags,
                    as 'rootsecret' and 'version' say: WARN on an OTP page not
                    locked, or one flash copy damaged; FAIL with no secret
        otp         the OTP rows SOS relies on (the security flags and the boot
                    key slots, never the root secret's): WARN on a row that
                    can't be read or whose error correction doesn't match, on
                    copies that disagree, and on a public development key in a
                    release build; FAIL when secure boot is on and this
                    firmware's key is in no valid slot (see 'bootkey')
        failcount   the count of wrong console passwords: WARN when it isn't
                    zero (the text gives the count)
        hostkey     the stored SSH host key is still the one sshd serves (its
                    fingerprint in the text); FAIL if the record changed
        card        /var/log and /certs on the mounted card, and the approved
                    TLS roots against /certs/roots.pem (see 'tls'): WARN if it
                    changed since approval. SKIP while anything else has the
                    card: unmounted by hand, sd check, a format, open files
    The slow checks run only on 'health full' or 'health run <name>':
        ram         a march test (March C-, six data patterns) over free heap,
                    taken a 4 KB block at a time while about 96 KB stays
                    free, and over the free 4 KB scratch bank, put back as it
                    was. A wrong read is tested again: WARN if it doesn't come
                    back, FAIL if the same address is wrong twice (power-cycle;
                    keys and seals are refused until a reset). Each wrong read
                    is logged with its address. Memory in use isn't tested
        image       the firmware in flash hashed (SHA-256) and compared with
                    the hash in the image, which the boot ROM checked at boot:
                    FAIL if the flash or the hash changed since (reflash: the
                    boot ROM won't start it again; keys and seals are refused
                    until a reset)
        sd-check    'sd check' on a card unmounted by hand ('sd unmount'): WARN
                    on problems, FAIL on clusters two files share or that are
                    used but marked free (the next write can overwrite a file;
                    'sd check fix' first). SKIP on a mounted card. The card
                    stays unmounted; any file command mounts it again

    A change of status is logged (facility kern): a FAIL at err, a WARN at
    warning, the way back to PASS at notice. A board that stays healthy logs
    one line per boot: the summary of the boot checks. A FAIL also refuses
    what depends on the check: making keys (the root secret, the owner secret,
    the SSH host key, an encrypted card, a console password's salt) or
    encrypting new data (saving the configuration). The refusal names the
    check, and the latest result counts: a check that passes again lifts it.
    Reading what is already stored is never refused. Only trng, crypto,
    crypto-ssh, ram and image refuse anything: a FAIL of the others says what
    the board is doing, and the feature's own command fixes it. The slow
    checks' results last until a reset, like their refusals.

    A fresh board whose root secret was refused this way keeps its console
    locked, saying which check failed; 'health' and 'reboot' work there.

    Lua scripts read the same results with sos.health() (see 'lua').

CONSOLE ONLY
    'health run' runs the boot and background checks now, one after the
    other, and prints each result as it comes; 'health run <name>' runs one
    check. 'health full' runs every check, including the slow ones (ram,
    image, sd-check: about a second). Ctrl-C stops: the check being run keeps
    its earlier result, those already done keep their new ones.

SEE ALSO
    uptime, glitch, mem, temp, power, dmesg, lua, config, rootsecret,
    bootkey, tls, time
@@ help
NAME
    help - list the commands this channel may use

SYNOPSIS
    help
    ?

DESCRIPTION
    Prints one line per command with a few words on each. Commands that only
    the console may run are listed only on the console. For the full story on
    a command, 'man <command>'.

SEE ALSO
    intro, man
@@ hostname
NAME
    hostname - show or set the board's hostname

SYNOPSIS
    hostname
    hostname <name>      (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    Shows the hostname, or sets it; the default is 'sos'. Saved; a new name
    takes effect at the next Wi-Fi connect ('wifi connect').

SEE ALSO
    net, wifi, config
@@ http
NAME
    http - HTTPS requests, for testing the client

SYNOPSIS
    http get <url>
    http head <url>
    http post <url> <text>

DESCRIPTION
    Makes one HTTPS request and prints the response: 'get' the status line and
    the body, then its size and timings and the server's certificate (its
    name, who issued it, and when it expires); 'head' the status line and
    headers; 'post' sends the text as text/plain (UTF-8) and prints the
    response as 'get' does. A job: Ctrl-C stops it. Quote a text that holds
    quotes, a '|' or '>' or repeated spaces, such as a JSON body: http post
    <url> '{"a":1}' (QUOTING in 'man intro').

    URLs must start with https:// and the path must already be
    percent-encoded (no spaces). The server's certificate must chain to a
    built-in root or one in the approved /certs/roots.pem; redirects aren't
    followed. What the server sends is filtered before it reaches the
    terminal: control characters show as '.', including the C1 range, bidi
    controls, zero-width and other invisible characters; other UTF-8 text
    shows as itself.

    Two HTTPS requests ('http' or a Lua script's sos.http, from any shells)
    can run at once; a third is refused until one finishes, and one more TLS
    connection is always kept free for time sync. A request is also refused
    while the heap is too low for a TLS handshake (about 64 KB free).

SEE ALSO
    tls, lua, mem
@@ intro
NAME
    intro - the SOS shell: where it runs, typing a line, what is console only

DESCRIPTION
    The same shell runs on the serial console (USB, mirrored on UART0) and in
    each SSH session. 'help' lists the commands this channel may use, 'man'
    lists these pages, and 'man <topic>' shows one.

    Console only means the command is refused over SSH. Anything that changes
    the configuration or other protected state needs physical access to the
    console. A command marked "console only to change" can still show its
    value from SSH.

    Typing a line: Enter runs it, Backspace deletes, Up and Down recall the
    commands typed in this session (kept in RAM only, never answers such as
    passwords; Down past the newest gives back what you were typing). Ctrl-C
    discards the line, cancels a question (setup, wifi set, ...) or stops a
    running job (ls, cat, cp, a Lua script). Ctrl-D on an empty line logs out.
    Commands typed while a job runs are kept and run after it.

    Tab completes the command name and the subcommand words after it ('time s'
    Tab Tab lists 'sync server'; 'wifi st' Tab gives 'wifi stats '). When the
    matches agree no further the terminal beeps, and a second Tab lists them;
    words only the console takes are offered only there. Some values are
    offered too: the sensitivity after 'glitch watch|on', 'https://' after
    'http get|head|post', the key numbers after 'key del', the saved networks'
    numbers after 'wifi remove|prefer', the NTS servers after 'nts ke' and
    'time server', those and the gateway after 'ping', and the pages on the
    card after 'man'.

    After a file command (ls, cd, cat, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, edit, more, lua) and
    after a '>' or '>>' on any line, Tab completes file names on the SD card
    ('cat do' Tab gives 'cat docs/', the next Tab goes on inside it); cd and
    mkdir offer directories only. After a '|' it completes the stage names
    (grep, head, ...). Tab reads the line as the shell does (QUOTING): a '|'
    or '>' inside quotes is text, and quoted words count as what they spell.
    A name with a space (or a quote, '\', '|', '>') is inserted escaped:
    'cat my' Tab gives 'cat my\ file.txt '; typed inside a quote it goes in as
    it is and the quote is left open for you to close: "cat 'my f" Tab gives
    "cat 'my file.txt". Upper and lower case don't matter, as on the card
    itself (ASCII letters only); names with control characters, with a space
    at either end or two in a row, aren't offered, and Tab doesn't mount the
    card. In a directory of more than 256 entries it lists what it read and
    says there is more.

    At the question for a Wi-Fi network's name ('wifi set', 'wifi add',
    setup), Tab completes the names of the networks around, strongest first,
    and a second Tab lists them with their signal. They come from a scan that
    starts when the question is asked and takes about half a second; until it
    ends Tab only lists what it has heard so far. Names with characters that
    can't be shown as they are aren't offered. At the chat prompt it completes
    /who, /quit and the names in the room. At the Lua prompt it completes
    names of globals and table fields ('string.fo'). Tab does nothing at a
    password or any other question.

    The console asks for its password before taking any command, once one is
    set, and locks itself again on logout, when the USB terminal disconnects
    and after 10 minutes with nothing typed; whatever it was running is ended.

    Paths given to the file commands are relative to the shell's working
    directory ('cd', 'pwd') unless they start with '/'. A name with spaces
    is given quoted or escaped ('cat "my file"', 'cp my\ file backup'), to
    the file commands as to tee and a '>'.

    Full-screen programs: 'edit <file>' edits a file, 'more <file>' reads one
    a screen at a time, and 'man <topic>' shows a page in the same viewer.
    Any command's output can be read that way too: end the line with '| more'
    ('dmesg | more', 'wifi stats | more'); the pager takes up to 32 KB of it.

    Pipes: a command's output can go through grep, head, tail and wc on its
    way, each after a '|' ('dmesg | grep wifi', 'ls | tail 3', 'cat x | grep
    error | more'). They work only after a '|'; 'man more' says how. A '>'
    and a path at the end of a line write the command's output to that file
    instead of the screen ('dmesg > /d.txt'); '>>' appends to it.

QUOTING
    A line is split into words at spaces, as a Unix shell does it, and the
    words go to the command with single spaces between them: 'echo a   b'
    prints "a b". Quotes make one word of what they hold, spaces included:
    in '...' everything is taken as it is; in "..." too, except that \" is a
    " and \\ is a \. Outside quotes a backslash takes the next character as
    it is ('a\ b' is one word). The quotes themselves don't reach the
    command: 'echo "a   b"' prints "a   b". The commands that take paths (ls,
    cd, cat, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, edit, more, lua) get each word whole, so a
    quoted path is one argument however many spaces it holds, and 'cat a b'
    is two (a usage error).

    '|', '>' and '>>' are always the shell's (a pipe, a redirect) unless they
    are quoted or after a backslash. So quote text that holds them, or an
    apostrophe, or repeated spaces:

        lua -e 'print("hi")'
        wall "don't panic"
        logger 'temp -> 40'
        echo 'a|b' > /x.txt

    Unquoted, 'wall don't' says "unmatched quote" and runs nothing, and a '>'
    in Lua code or an '->' in a message would write a file. Nothing else is
    special: no variables, wildcards, ~, ;, & or #.

SEE ALSO
    help, man, logout, passwd, setup, edit, more, grep
@@ key
NAME
    key - the SSH public keys the board trusts

SYNOPSIS
    key
    key list
    key add [<line>]      (console only)
    key del <n>           (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    'key list' numbers the trusted keys and shows their fingerprints. They are
    what decides who may log in over SSH (and SFTP): any user name logs in
    with a trusted key, and the name is only shown. The key's comment names
    the person in 'who', 'wall' and the chat.

    'key add' trusts one authorized_keys line (ssh-ed25519 or
    ecdsa-sha2-nistp256; up to 8 keys); without a line it asks for one, to
    paste. 'key del <n>' stops trusting key n. Changes apply at once: a new
    key works for the next login.

    A line given after 'key add' goes through the shell's QUOTING ('man
    intro'): a quote, backslash, '|' or '>' in the key's comment must be
    escaped with a backslash. A line pasted at the question is taken as it
    is.

SEE ALSO
    ssh, setup, who, config
@@ logger
NAME
    logger - add a line to the system log

SYNOPSIS
    logger [-p <level>] <message>

DESCRIPTION
    Adds the message to the log under facility 'user', at level 'notice'
    unless -p gives err, warning, notice, info or debug. The line says which
    shell it came from: 'logger (console): ...' or 'logger (ssh <name>): ...',
    the name as 'who' shows it. At most 10 a second; beyond that it says the
    message wasn't logged. Lua scripts log the same way with sos.log().

    Quote a message that holds an apostrophe, a '|' or '>' (an '->' too) or
    repeated spaces: logger "it's 40 -> 41" (QUOTING in 'man intro').

SEE ALSO
    dmesg, log, who
@@ logout
NAME
    logout - end this session, or lock the console

SYNOPSIS
    logout
    exit
    Ctrl-D (on an empty line)

DESCRIPTION
    Over SSH, ends the session. On the console, locks it: it asks for the
    console password again before taking any command (not possible while no
    password is set). The console also locks itself when the USB terminal
    stays disconnected for a second (the terminal program drops DTR or the
    cable is unplugged; a shorter drop, as around a port being reopened,
    doesn't lock) and after 10 minutes with nothing typed. Either lock ends
    whatever the console was running: a copy, 'sd test', a Lua script, the
    chat, the editor (unsaved text is lost). The command history of the
    session is wiped.

SEE ALSO
    passwd, intro
@@ log
NAME
    log - the log levels, the log files on the card and their rotation

SYNOPSIS
    log
    log console <level>           (console only)
    log history <level>           (console only)
    log file <level>              (console only)
    log rotate                    (console only)
    log rotate every <N>d         (console only)
    log rotate at <HH:MM>         (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    Three levels decide what the log does with a message: 'console' is what
    the serial console prints as it happens (default notice), 'history' what
    is kept in memory for 'dmesg' (default info), 'file' what is written to
    the log files on the card (default info). Levels, from most to least
    important: err, warning, notice, info, debug. A level change lasts until
    reboot and is itself logged (facility auth) before it applies.

    'log' shows the levels and the log files' state: writing, no card mounted
    (messages wait in the history), or stopped after a failed write (with the
    file and the error); lines and bytes written this boot, bytes waiting to
    be written, messages dropped from the history before they could be
    written; and the rotation: how often and when, the last and the next.

    Every message the history keeps is also written to the card, one file per
    facility: /var/log/kern.log, auth.log, daemon.log, user.log, and syslog
    for messages without a facility. Lines read

        2026-11-26T14:03:12.345Z notice console: logged in

    (UTC; '+12.345', seconds since boot, while the clock isn't set), and each
    boot starts with a '--- boot: SOS <commit>, last reset: <why> ---' line.
    Warnings and errors are written at once, the rest within 5 s. Without a
    card, messages wait in the 4 KB history and a mount is tried every minute
    (not after 'sd unmount'); once a card is in they are written, with a line
    saying how many were dropped meanwhile. A failed write stops the files
    until the card is mounted again.

    Rotation: every 7 days at 00:00 UTC by default, each file becomes
    <name>.1 (replacing the previous one) and a new one starts, so the card
    holds the current period and the one before. 'log rotate' rotates now;
    'log rotate every <N>d' (1 to 365) and 'log rotate at <HH:MM>' change
    when, saved in the configuration. The time of the last rotation is kept
    on the card (/var/log/.rotated), so a reboot keeps the count and a
    rotation missed while the board was off happens at the first chance;
    without the clock set nothing rotates.

    Like /certs, /var/log can be read from anywhere (SSH, SFTP, Lua) but
    changed only on the console, so a session that gets in can't erase its
    traces; /var itself is protected too.

SEE ALSO
    dmesg, logger, sd
@@ ls
NAME
    ls - list a directory on the SD card

SYNOPSIS
    ls [<path>]

DESCRIPTION
    Lists the directory (the working directory without a path): each entry
    with its size and date. A job: it does a little work per pass of the main
    loop, so the console, SSH and the watchdog keep going, and Ctrl-C stops it.
    Files are dated with the system clock (UTC). A file that is still being
    written ('tee', '>', 'cp', an SFTP upload) and hasn't been synced yet
    shows size 0, with 'being written' in place of the date (on an exFAT card)
    or the time it was opened (FAT32 stores that at once); after a sync, the
    size and date as of that sync.

    Cards over 2 GB with exFAT or FAT32 work; the card is mounted when first
    used. Paths are relative to the shell's working directory unless they
    start with '/'. A name with spaces is quoted: ls 'my dir'.

SEE ALSO
    cd, cat, sd, intro
@@ lua
NAME
    lua - run Lua scripts on the second core

SYNOPSIS
    lua <file>
    lua -e <code>
    lua
    lua -b <file>
    lua ps
    lua stop

DESCRIPTION
    Lua 5.4 runs on core 1, so a script can compute flat out without slowing
    the console, SSH or the watchdog. 'lua <file>' runs a script from the card
    (a relative path starts at the working directory); 'lua -e <code>' runs
    the code given; 'lua' opens a prompt where each line runs as entered, in
    one state for the session (an expression's value is printed; Ctrl-C
    interrupts a line; Ctrl-D on an empty line or 'exit' leaves; Tab completes
    the name being typed, 'string.fo' to 'string.format', looking into tables
    without running any of the script's code). The output goes to the shell
    that started the script, and Ctrl-C stops it, as does ending the session.
    One script runs at a time.

    Quote the code after -e with '...': the shell takes quotes, '|' and '>'
    for itself (QUOTING in 'man intro'). So: lua -e 'print("hi")', and
    lua -e 'print(2 > 1)'. Unquoted words after -e are joined with single
    spaces (lua -e print(1 + 1) works too). A file name is one word, quoted
    if it holds spaces: lua 'my script.lua'.

    'lua -b <file>' runs a script in the background with no shell: its output
    goes to the log, a line at a time (dmesg; at most 10 lines a second, the
    rest counted as dropped), and its end is logged. 'lua ps' shows the
    running script, 'lua stop' stops it wherever it runs. If the card has
    /autorun.lua it starts in the background a few seconds after boot, except
    after a crash or watchdog reset (then start it by hand). Use 'lua ./ps' to
    run a file called ps.

    Scripts have the base, string, table, math, utf8 and coroutine libraries,
    'require' (modules from /lib and the working directory, Lua source only),
    and the 'sos' library: sos.read/write/append/list/remove/mkdir,
    rename/copy/stat/exists and sos.sd.info for files; sos.time, sos.uptime,
    sos.date (os.date's formats, in UTC), sos.localdate, sos.localtime and
    sos.zone (the same in the board's time zone, 'time zone'), sos.clock,
    sos.clock_sync, sos.who, sos.ssh.status, sos.tls.roots, sos.wall,
    sos.random, sos.sleep, sos.log; timers sos.every/after/cancel (a
    background script keeps going while timers are left); sos.wifi.scan,
    sos.net.resolve and sos.ping; sos.http.get/post/head/request and
    sos.http.download (into a file) for HTTPS (the response includes how long
    the request took); tables about the board: sos.sensors, sos.mem,
    sos.version, sos.reset_reason, sos.glitch, sos.wifi.status and sos.health
    (the self-checks' latest results); sos.dmesg for the system log (with a
    cursor, for only the newer lines); sos.hash, sos.hmac (SHA-1, SHA-2),
    sos.base64 and sos.json; and sos.reboot([why]), refused until the board
    has been up 10 minutes. There is no io, os or debug. Failures return nil
    and a message. Each script may allocate up to 128 KB. Full details:
    docs/lua.md in the repository.

SEE ALSO
    dmesg, logger, http, mem, health
@@ man
NAME
    man - the manual: list the pages, read one, or install them

SYNOPSIS
    man
    man <topic>
    man install

DESCRIPTION
    'man' lists the pages on the card with a line on each; 'man <topic>' shows
    one in the pager (q quits, h lists the keys, / searches). A topic is a
    command name ('man wifi'); 'man intro' is a place to start. Tab after
    'man' completes the topics of the pages on the card.

    Piped, 'man <topic>' prints the page as plain text instead of opening the
    pager, like 'cat': 'man wifi | head 12', 'man wifi | grep -n channel'.
    ('man wifi | more' pages the text as any command's output.) 'man' alone
    and 'man install' are not affected.

    The pages live on the SD card as /man/<topic>.txt; the firmware holds
    none. When there are none, 'man' offers to download them. 'man install'
    downloads them from the SOS repository on GitHub over HTTPS (the board
    needs the network): one file, man/pages.bundle, from the commit this
    firmware was built from so the pages match it, or from main
    if that commit's pages aren't on GitHub. Each page is written to
    /man/<topic>.~new and renamed into place when complete, so a download
    that fails midway leaves the earlier pages whole. The other way is
    tools/man-install.sh <host> on the development machine, which copies the
    pages over SFTP.

SEE ALSO
    help, more, intro
@@ mem
NAME
    mem - heap and stack use, by feature

SYNOPSIS
    mem

DESCRIPTION
    The heap: in use, peak and total, then what each feature holds and its
    peak (ssh/tls, lua, editor, files, http, time, ping, wifi, health, zlib,
    and 'other' for the rest), lwIP's own pool, and core 0's peak stack use.
    After a Lua script has run, also how deep the last script went into core
    1's 32 KB stack.

    The heap is about 345 KB and is shared: an SSH session uses up to ~100 KB
    while transferring, a TLS handshake needs about 64 KB free, a Lua script
    may take 128 KB, the editor takes the file plus 32 KB of room, gunzip
    or zcat about 50 KB, and gzip about 65 KB.

SEE ALSO
    ssh, lua, edit
@@ mkdir
NAME
    mkdir - create a directory

SYNOPSIS
    mkdir <dir>

DESCRIPTION
    Creates the directory. Only the console may create /certs, which holds the
    extra TLS root certificates ('tls roots').

SEE ALSO
    rm, ls, tls
@@ more
NAME
    more - read a file a screen at a time, whatever its size

SYNOPSIS
    more <file>
    <command> | more

DESCRIPTION
    Shows the file a screen at a time, on the same kind of terminal as 'edit'
    and with the same screen size handling (Ctrl-L after resizing). The file
    can be of any size: only a 16 KB window of it is in memory, read from the
    card as you move. Nothing in a file can send the terminal a sequence:
    controls, invalid UTF-8 and invisible or bidi characters show as '?', and
    long lines scroll sideways.

    Piped, 'more <file>' prints the file as plain text, as 'cat <file>' does,
    instead of opening the pager: 'more /log/sos.log | tail 5'.

    The bottom row shows the file name, the lines on screen ('lines 12-34',
    with the total once it is known: after G, or once the end has been on
    screen) and how far in you are, or END.

KEYS
    Space, f, PgDn / b, PgUp    a screen forward / back
    d / u                       half a screen forward / back
    j, Enter, Down / k, Up      a line forward / back
    g, Home / G, End            the start / the end of the file (the first G
                                on a large file counts its lines, about a
                                second per megabyte)
    Left / Right                half a screen sideways, for long lines
    / (Ctrl-F)                  find: type the text, Enter finds the next
                                match from the line after the top one (empty:
                                the last search again), wrapping around to
                                the start; Ctrl-C cancels
    n                           the next match
    F                           follow: stay on the end as output arrives
                                ('<command> | more' while the command runs;
                                any movement key leaves it)
    Ctrl-L                      redraw, and ask the terminal for its size
    h, ?                        list the keys
    q (Ctrl-Q, Ctrl-C)          quit (Ctrl-C first stops a command that is
                                still running: see NOTES)

NOTES
    '<command> | more' runs the command with its output kept instead of
    printed, and shows it here ('dmesg | more', 'ls | more', 'wifi stats chip
    | more'). A command that runs for a while ('cat big.log | more', 'ping
    host | more') is shown as soon as it has printed something, and its output
    keeps arriving while you read: the bottom row shows '...' for the total
    and 'END so far' at its end until the command has finished, and F follows
    the end. While it runs, q ends the command and leaves, and Ctrl-C stops
    it and keeps what it printed ('stopped' in the bottom row; Ctrl-C again,
    or q, leaves). Up to 32 KB of output is kept; then the command is
    stopped and the text ends with a note saying so. A command that asks a
    question runs as if there were no '| more' (if it asks while being
    shown, the pager leaves and its output so far is printed above the
    question), and a full-screen program (edit, more, man) ignores it.
    Ctrl-C before the command has printed anything drops it, as does
    anything typed before the pager opens (it would be keys for the pager).
    A command with no output says so instead of opening the pager. Messages
    from other shells wait until the pager is left.

PIPES
    A command's output can go through filters on its way to the screen or to
    the pager, one after another, each after a '|':

        dmesg | grep wifi
        cat /log/sos.log | grep -i error | tail 20 | more
        help | wc

    The filters are grep (lines with a text), head (the first lines; it
    stops the command once it has them), tail (the last lines) and wc
    (counts); see their pages. 'tee [-a] <path>' also writes what passes it
    to a file ('dmesg | tee /dmesg.txt | more'). 'more' can only come last,
    and at most four filters fit in a line. The output streams through:
    grep shows lines as the command prints them, and with '| more' only what
    the filters let through is kept, so 'cat big.log | grep error | more'
    isn't cut at 32 KB unless the matches are. When the 32 KB are full the
    command is stopped,
    as head stops it.

    A '|' is always a pipe, and the word after it must be a stage name
    (grep, head, tail, wc, tee, more): 'ls | morex' says "morex: not a
    filter" and runs nothing. A '|' that is text is quoted ('echo "a|b"';
    QUOTING in 'man intro'). A line may end with '> <path>' (or '>>'), which
    writes what the stages let through to the file instead of the screen, as
    a tee that passes nothing on ('man tee'); it can't go with '| more'. A
    stage that doesn't parse ('x | grep', 'x | head y') prints its usage and
    the command doesn't run. Ctrl-C drops
    what the filters hold (tail's lines, wc's count); a command that asks a
    question or draws its own screen runs as if there were no pipe. Tab
    completes the stage names after '| ', and grep's options.

    'man <topic>' shows a manual page in this viewer (piped, it prints the
    page as text, like 'more <file>'). Log messages are held on
    the console while it runs there, as for the editor.

SEE ALSO
    edit, cat, man, grep, head, tail, wc
@@ mv
NAME
    mv - move or rename a file or directory

SYNOPSIS
    mv <from> <to>

DESCRIPTION
    Moves or renames. Moving to a directory keeps the name. A file a running
    command has open can't be moved. Only the console may move anything into
    or out of /certs.

SEE ALSO
    cp, rm, ls
@@ net
NAME
    net - Wi-Fi link state, address and ARP counters

SYNOPSIS
    net

DESCRIPTION
    The Wi-Fi link state and signal strength (RSSI), the IP address, gateway
    and hostname, and the ARP packets received and sent with the keepalive
    state. While connected the board announces its address once a minute (a
    gratuitous ARP), without which a device that sends nothing stops receiving
    broadcasts and clients get "no route to host" once the router forgets it;
    'wifi keepalive off' turns the announcement off until reboot, for testing.

SEE ALSO
    wifi, hostname, ssh
@@ nts
NAME
    nts - NTS state, and its two steps run by hand

SYNOPSIS
    nts
    nts ke
    nts ke <host>      (console only)
    nts query

DESCRIPTION
    For diagnosing time sync. 'nts' shows the last key exchange (cookies held,
    the NTP server it named) and the last query. 'nts ke' runs a key exchange
    with the build-time default server (not the one set with 'time server');
    'nts ke <host>' with another server. 'nts query' sends one authenticated
    NTS time query; 'nts' then shows the offset. None of these sets the clock:
    'time sync' does.

SEE ALSO
    time, tls
@@ passwd
NAME
    passwd - set or change the console password

SYNOPSIS
    passwd        (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    Sets the console password, or changes it after asking for the current one:
    8 to 64 printable ASCII characters, asked twice, not echoed. A board with
    no console password opens straight into setting one (a new board: the
    whole of 'setup', which starts with it). SSH sessions aren't affected;
    they log in with their keys.

    The console asks for the password before taking any command, and until it
    is typed shows nothing: no greeting, no log lines, no messages. Each check
    takes about 0.3 s by design, and after a wrong answer the next one waits
    1 s, 2 s, 4 s ... up to 30 s; the count is kept in flash, so unplugging the
    board between guesses skips no wait. An answer interrupted with Ctrl-C
    counts as wrong. Wrong attempts are logged.

    What is stored is not the password but a verifier in the sealed
    configuration (PBKDF2, keyed under the root secret). If the password is
    forgotten there is no way round it: erase the configuration over USB (or
    provision the board again), which locks the board's encrypted SD cards.

SEE ALSO
    logout, setup, config
@@ ping
NAME
    ping - is a host reachable?

SYNOPSIS
    ping <host> [<count>]

DESCRIPTION
    Sends ICMP echo requests to a name or an IPv4 address, one a second: 4 of
    them, or <count> (1 to 65535). Each reply prints a line with the address
    it came from, its sequence number, TTL and round-trip time:

        reply from 192.168.1.1: seq=1 ttl=64 time=4.2 ms

    A request with no reply within the second prints 'seq=2 timeout', and a
    later reply to it is ignored. If a router or the host sends back an ICMP
    error about a request instead (destination unreachable, time exceeded),
    that prints in its place and the request counts as lost:

        error from 192.168.1.1: seq=2 host unreachable

    A host on the LAN that is off or missing sends nothing, so that is still a
    timeout. Then a summary: sent, received, loss, how many ICMP errors came
    back, and the lowest, average and highest round-trip time. A job: Ctrl-C
    stops it and prints the summary. Every shell can run one at the same time,
    and the board still answers pings itself meanwhile. Refused while Wi-Fi
    isn't connected.

SEE ALSO
    net, wifi
@@ power
NAME
    power - the supply voltage, and whether USB power is present

SYNOPSIS
    power

DESCRIPTION
    The supply voltage (VSYS, the board's input) and whether USB power is
    present, with the lowest and highest since boot and how long ago each was.
    On USB power VSYS is the USB voltage less a diode's drop (about 0.35 V); a
    battery or other supply feeds it directly. Within a few percent. A reading
    taken during a Wi-Fi transmission catches its dip (about 0.13 V on USB
    power), so the lowest can be a brief one. Read every 10 s and logged with
    the temperature (see 'temp'); the glitch detector's report includes the
    last reading before a detector reset.

SEE ALSO
    temp, glitch
@@ pwd
NAME
    pwd - show the working directory

SYNOPSIS
    pwd

DESCRIPTION
    Prints this shell's working directory.

SEE ALSO
    cd, ls
@@ reboot
NAME
    reboot - restart the device

SYNOPSIS
    reboot

DESCRIPTION
    Restarts the board at once. The configuration is in flash and the clock
    comes back from the RTC, so nothing is lost but SSH sessions, running jobs
    and the chat. A reboot doesn't count as a watchdog or fault reset in
    'uptime'. After a crash or watchdog reset, '/autorun.lua' is skipped until
    the next normal boot, which a reboot is.

SEE ALSO
    uptime, lua
@@ rm
NAME
    rm - delete a file or an empty directory

SYNOPSIS
    rm <path>

DESCRIPTION
    Deletes the file, or the directory if it is empty. A file a running
    command has open can't be deleted. Only the console may delete anything
    under /certs.

SEE ALSO
    mkdir, mv, ls
@@ rootsecret
NAME
    rootsecret - the root secret the keys protecting stored data derive from

SYNOPSIS
    rootsecret
    rootsecret forget      (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    The saved configuration and the SSH host key are sealed under keys derived
    from the board's root secret, and so is the key that unlocks the board's
    encrypted SD cards. 'rootsecret' says what state it is in and where it is:

        in OTP            with whether its page is locked for this boot
        in flash          an open board; not protected from someone who has
                          the board
        none              and why: a console-only board with none in OTP never
                          uses its flash for one
        unusable          present but damaged, or from newer firmware; kept as
                          it is

    It is stored twice; if one copy is damaged the other is used and this says
    so.

    'rootsecret forget' clears a record that can't be used, after you type
    'yes': its whole flash sector is erased and the next boot makes a new one.
    Refused while the root secret is usable, and always on a console-only
    board. A configuration and host key sealed under the old one can't be read
    again.

SEE ALSO
    version, config, bootkey
@@ sd
NAME
    sd - the SD card: information, speed, unmounting, checks, benchmark, format

SYNOPSIS
    sd
    sd info
    sd speed [<MHz>]                      (change: console only)
    sd unmount                            (console only)
    sd check                              (console only)
    sd check fix                          (console only)
    sd test [<KB> [keep]]                 (console only)
    sd format [exfat | fat32] [plain]     (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    'sd' (or 'sd info') shows the card and volume information, the SPI clock,
    and whether the card is encrypted, with its card id. 'sd speed' shows the
    SPI clock, set and actual; 'sd speed <MHz>' sets it (1 to 75) and
    reinitializes the card; not saved.

    'sd unmount' unmounts the card before it is taken out; refused while a
    file is open. No card-detect switch is wired by default, so the board
    doesn't notice a swap: unmount, swap, and the next file command mounts
    the new card.

    'sd check' checks the card's file system (exFAT or FAT32) for damage,
    such as a power cut during a write leaves: lost clusters, files whose
    size doesn't match their clusters, clusters two files share, broken
    directory entries. It runs only on an unmounted card: run 'sd unmount'
    first. While it runs nothing else uses the card: file commands, SFTP,
    Lua fail as if there were no card, TLS roots not loaded yet can't be
    read (roots already in memory still work), the log files wait, and the
    other 'sd' commands refuse. It prints one line per problem, then
    a summary, which also goes to the log. It never writes to the card;
    'sd check fix' repairs. Ctrl-C stops it. Afterwards the card is
    left as 'sd unmount' leaves it: the next file command mounts it.
    The report can't go to a file on the card: 'sd check > file' opens the
    file first, which mounts the card, so the check refuses ('the card is
    mounted') and that line is what lands in the file. 'sd check | more'
    works.

    The report starts with 'checking exFAT, <size>, <n> clusters of <size>,
    encrypted (<n> passes)', then 'pass <i>/<n>' lines; the clusters are
    checked a window at a time, one pass over the directories per window.
    Problems start with the path when there is one ('/a/b.txt: chain
    loops'). Lines about clusters: 'lost: clusters <a>-<b> (<size>)' (in
    use, reached by no file), 'reached but free: ...' (exFAT: used by a
    file but free in the allocation bitmap), '<path>: cross-linked with an
    earlier entry', and on exFAT '<n> clusters used by more than one entry'
    when no file can be named. exFAT also has 'up-case table checksum ...'
    and 'the allocation bitmap's chain isn't contiguous ...' (FatFs won't
    mount such a card). Lines starting 'note:' aren't problems: a chain
    longer than its file, a stale free count, the volume flagged dirty or
    as having had a disk error (left by a PC or camera; also in the
    summary), FAT entry 0 not matching the media byte, an up-case table
    unlike FatFs's. A card that can't be checked at all ends with 'not
    checked: <why>' (no card, no volume, a boot sector FatFs would refuse).

    'sd check fix' checks the same way, then repairs what can be repaired
    without losing data a file still reaches: lost clusters are freed,
    chains longer than their file are cut to the file's size (the rest
    freed), a second FAT that differs from the first gets the first's
    sectors, and FSInfo's free count is set to unknown so that the next
    mount counts it again. On exFAT the allocation bitmap is what's
    repaired: bits of lost clusters are cleared, and clusters a file uses
    but the bitmap calls free are marked in use. It never touches cross-linked
    files, broken or short chains, damaged directory entries or names, the
    boot sector or the partition table: each of those means choosing whose
    data wins, which is for a PC and a backup. With cross-links on the card
    it doesn't cut chains either ('trims skipped: cross-linked files, fix
    those on a PC first'), and when part of the directory tree couldn't be
    read it frees and cuts nothing, since what that part holds would look
    lost. An empty file that still has clusters is left as it is.

    After the check it shows the plan: 'to repair:' with a line per kind
    and its count, 'left alone:' with what it skips and why, and asks for
    'yes', as 'sd format' does. Anything else ends it with 'not repaired',
    and nothing has been written. A plan with nothing to write
    ends with 'nothing to repair' instead. On 'yes' it repairs, one sector
    at a time, then checks the card again: 'repaired: <what>; <n> sectors
    written', then 'after repair: <summary>'. The plan, what was written
    and the summary go to the log ('sd: check fix: ...').

    It can stop at any sector, by Ctrl-C, a write error or a power cut,
    and the card is no worse than before: files read the same bytes, and a
    check finds the problems it found before or fewer, plus at most a note
    that the two FATs differ and lost clusters at the end of a chain being
    cut (space, not data: 'sd check fix' again frees them). A write error
    ends it with 'stopped: write error at sector <n>; the card is no worse
    than before (sd check shows what is left)'. A card that lost power
    during a write can still hold a half-written sector, which no order of
    writes prevents: check it again before use. Like 'sd check', it is
    console only, runs only on an unmounted card, and nothing else uses the
    card until it ends, the question included.

    'sd test [<KB> [keep]]' is a write and read benchmark: 4 to 65536 KB,
    default 256; everything else waits until it finishes. 'keep' leaves
    /sos-test.txt on the card.

    'sd format' erases the whole card (all partitions) and formats it with
    one partition, exFAT by default, after you type 'yes'. The card is
    ENCRYPTED (XTS-AES-128, with a new key this board keeps) unless 'plain'
    is given; a PC sees an encrypted card as unformatted. When an encrypted
    format finishes it shows the card's id and data key, ONCE: store the key
    offline, it is the only way to read the card if the board is lost or its
    configuration erased (tools/host/sdcrypt.c, or cryptsetup). It can't be
    shown again later. Old files on the card aren't erased: what was written
    unencrypted before stays readable until overwritten. SSH sessions pause
    until the format finishes.

    An encrypted card's key is stored on the card, wrapped with a key made
    from the board's root secret and the owner secret in its configuration,
    so the card unlocks at boot with nobody logged in and survives
    reflashing. Erasing the configuration ('config reset', provisioning,
    erasing over USB) locks the board's cards: that is deliberate. A card made
    by another board is refused; 'sd format' erases it for use here.
    Encryption is software AES: about 1.1 MB/s write and 1.0 MB/s read,
    against 2.0 / 1.7 MB/s unencrypted.

SEE ALSO
    ls, config, rootsecret
@@ setup
NAME
    setup - guided configuration of a new board

SYNOPSIS
    setup        (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    Asks, in order: a new console password if none is set (8 to 64 characters,
    not echoed, twice), the Wi-Fi network (SSID; Tab offers the networks
    around) and its password (not echoed;
    empty for an open network), and a public key to trust (paste one
    authorized_keys line, e.g. ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub). Enter keeps the value
    shown in brackets; Ctrl-C cancels without changing anything.

    Each answer is saved in flash at once. Wi-Fi connects in the background,
    and once the board has a network and a trusted key the SSH server starts;
    the log shows the address and the host key fingerprint to check against
    what your client prints.

    The same settings can be written from the development machine over USB
    (ninja provision), which replaces everything saved and makes the board a
    new owner.

SEE ALSO
    passwd, wifi, key, config, ssh
@@ sha1sum
NAME
    sha1sum - print or check SHA-1 checksums of files

SYNOPSIS
    sha1sum <file>...
    sha1sum -c [--ignore-missing] <list>

DESCRIPTION
    Like sha256sum, with SHA-1: 40 hex digits, and BSD list lines start
    with 'SHA1 ('. 'man sha256sum' describes the forms, the list format and
    the results.

    SHA-1 is broken for collisions: someone who makes a file can make
    another with the same SHA-1. sha1sum is here for old published lists;
    don't rely on it for files from others. Use sha256sum where the list
    offers it.

SEE ALSO
    sha256sum, sha512sum
@@ sha256sum
NAME
    sha256sum - print or check SHA-256 checksums of files

SYNOPSIS
    sha256sum <file>...
    sha256sum -c [--ignore-missing] <list>

DESCRIPTION
    Prints each file's SHA-256 and its name, one line per file, as GNU
    sha256sum does:

        e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855  empty

    The name is printed as given, relative to the working directory. A file
    that can't be read says why ('sha256sum: <name>: no such file') and the
    others go on. 'sha256sum *.tar.gz > SHA256SUMS' needs the names written
    out (the shell has no wildcards), and the list it makes is one '-c'
    reads. sha1sum and sha512sum work the same way with their algorithms.

    -c <list> reads a list of checksums and checks each file it names,
    one line per file:

        pkg-1.2.tar.gz: OK
        other.bin: FAILED
        old.tgz: missing
        ../etc/x: unsafe path

    and a last line with the counts: '2 OK, 1 FAILED, 1 missing'. The shell
    has no exit status, so that line is the result: anything other than
    'OK' means a file wasn't verified. 'unreadable' counts files that exist
    but couldn't be read, 'unsafe' names refused, and 'improperly formatted'
    lines that aren't a checksum line (they aren't printed one by one). A
    list with no checksum line for this algorithm says 'no checksums
    found'.

    --ignore-missing (with -c) skips files that aren't on the card and
    leaves them out of the counts: for a SHA256SUMS that lists the files of
    every platform when only one was downloaded. When none of the files is
    there, it says 'no file was verified'.

LIST FORMAT
    Each line is one of:

        <hex>  <name>              GNU, as sha256sum prints it
        <hex> *<name>              GNU binary mode (the same on SOS)
        SHA256 (<name>) = <hex>    BSD ('shasum --tag', 'openssl dgst')

    The hex may be upper or lower case and must be the algorithm's length
    (64 digits for SHA-256). A BSD line must name this command's algorithm:
    a 'SHA1 (...)' line counts as improperly formatted for sha256sum.
    Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are skipped ('#' after spaces
    counts as improperly formatted, as in GNU). A line may be up
    to 4 KB long ('line <n>: too long' otherwise); lists written on Windows
    (CR LF) work.

    The names are relative to the list's directory, not the working
    directory: 'sha256sum -c /apps/SHA256SUMS' checks the files in /apps
    from anywhere. A name that is absolute or holds '..' is refused
    ('<name>: unsafe path'), so a list from a server can't make the board
    read outside the list's directory. So a list made with absolute names
    ('sha256sum /apps/f > f.sha256') isn't checked: cd to the directory
    first and give relative names ('sha256sum f > f.sha256').

NAMES
    A name holding a newline or a backslash is printed the GNU way: the
    line starts with '\' and the name has '\n' for the newline and '\\' for
    the backslash. '-c' reads that form back. A name with another control
    character isn't hashed ('<name>: name has control characters'), since
    the line would go to the terminal; in '-c' output such characters are
    shown as '?'. A file named like an option is given after '--':
    'sha256sum -- -c'.

NOTES
    A job: one file at a time, 4 KB read and hashed per step, so other
    shells and SSH keep going; Ctrl-C stops it ('-c' then says 'stopped'
    instead of the counts). Hashing runs in software (wolfCrypt), not on
    the RP2350's SHA-256 block.

SEE ALSO
    sha1sum, sha512sum, cat, ls, http
@@ sha512sum
NAME
    sha512sum - print or check SHA-512 checksums of files

SYNOPSIS
    sha512sum <file>...
    sha512sum -c [--ignore-missing] <list>

DESCRIPTION
    Like sha256sum, with SHA-512: 128 hex digits, and BSD list lines start
    with 'SHA512 ('. 'man sha256sum' describes the forms, the list format
    and the results. SHA-512 runs slower than SHA-256 on the board.

SEE ALSO
    sha256sum, sha1sum
@@ ssh
NAME
    ssh - the SSH server: host key, sessions and refused connections

SYNOPSIS
    ssh

DESCRIPTION
    The host key fingerprint, the sessions in use and connections refused
    because all were busy, connections refused from a penalized address or
    dropped before login, logins so far, and each session: user and address,
    state, shell or SFTP, how long. Yours is marked '*'. The longest single
    SSH work step is shown too, a guide to the watchdog's margin.

    The server starts once the board has a Wi-Fi network and at least one
    trusted key. Only public-key login is accepted, with one of the keys 'key
    list' shows; the user name doesn't matter and is only displayed. Up to 3
    sessions at once; a connection has 30 s to log in (5 s while every session
    is busy), and an address whose connections end without a login 30 times
    within a minute is refused until it has stayed away for a minute.

    The Ed25519 host key is made when the board is first set up and sealed in
    flash for the board's owner: erasing the configuration ('config reset',
    provisioning, erasing over USB) makes it unreadable and the next setup
    makes a new one, so SSH clients warn that the host key changed. That is
    intended: a wiped board set up again is a new identity.

    SFTP is served on the same port with the same login, rooted at the card.

SEE ALSO
    key, who, config, net
@@ tail
NAME
    tail - the last lines of a command's output

SYNOPSIS
    <command> | tail [<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    Shows the last n lines of the command's output (10 without a number; 1 to
    999999) once the command has finished ('ls | tail 3', 'dmesg | tail').
    It keeps at most 4 KB: when the lines asked for are longer than that, it
    shows the last whole lines that fit, and says nothing about it. A single
    line longer than 4 KB shows its end. A last line without a newline is
    counted as a line, as it came.

    Ctrl-C while the command runs drops what tail holds: nothing is shown.

NOTES
    tail works only after a '|': typed on its own it says so. More about
    pipes in 'man more'.

SEE ALSO
    head, grep, wc, more
@@ tar
NAME
    tar - list, extract and make tar archives on the card

SYNOPSIS
    tar tf <archive>
    tar xf <archive> [-C <dir>] [-f]
    tar cf <archive> [-C <dir>] [-f] <path>...
    tar czf <archive> [-C <dir>] [-f] <path>...

DESCRIPTION
    'tar tf' lists an archive; 'tar xf' extracts it into the working
    directory, or into <dir> with -C (which must exist). Plain .tar,
    .tar.gz and .tgz are all read the same way: gzip is recognised by the
    file's first bytes and decompressed in the same pass, with no
    temporary .tar. Reading never changes the archive.

    'tar cf' makes an archive of files and directories; 'tar czf' makes it
    gzipped (.tar.gz or .tgz, as you name it).

    The mode word is written as on Unix: tf, xf, cf, and also -tf, -xf,
    xzf, tzf, czf; z is needed only to make a gzipped archive, and v (xvf,
    cvf) prints each name as it is extracted or added.
    Archives made by GNU tar, bsdtar and others are read: ustar, GNU (long
    names), pax (long names and sizes) and old v7 headers.

    'tar tf' prints a line per entry: its type (- file, d directory, l
    symlink, h hard link, c b p devices and FIFOs, ? other), size, date
    (UTC, as 'ls' shows dates) and name, and a link's target. An entry
    that 'tar xf' would skip has the reason after it in brackets. It goes
    through pipes: 'tar tf pkg.tgz | grep README', 'tar tf x.tar | more'.

SAFETY
    An archive comes from elsewhere, so every name is checked before
    anything is created; an entry that fails is skipped, said, and
    counted, and extraction goes on:
    - a leading / is removed (said once): names stay under the target
    - a name with '..' in it: 'unsafe path'
    - a name FAT can't store as given ("*:<>?|\, control characters,
      ending in a space or a dot, over 255 characters): 'not a valid FAT
      name' or 'name too long'
    - FAT ignores case: a name that differs only in case from one written
      in this run is skipped ('same name as <other> on FAT'). The same
      name twice: the later one wins, as on Unix
    - links (FAT has none), devices and FIFOs are skipped, never written
      as files, never followed
    - protected paths (/certs, /var/log) only from the console, as for cp
    - the archive itself is never written over
    The names in long-name and pax records follow the same rules.

WRITING
    Each file is written to '<name>.part' and renamed at its end, so an
    interrupted extraction leaves no half-written file under a real name.
    Directories are made as needed. Files and directories get the date
    stored in the archive; modes and owners are ignored.

    An existing file is not replaced: it is skipped with 'exists (-f
    replaces it)'. Note that -f here means overwrite, unlike Unix tar,
    where -f names the archive (here the archive is always the word after
    the mode). With -f the old file is moved aside to '<name>.old' until
    the new one has its name. An existing directory is merged into.

    At the end: '<n> files, <n> directories, <n> skipped'. Ctrl-C, a read
    error, a full card or a damaged archive stops it with 'stopped:
    <why>': the current .part is removed and what was extracted stays.
    An archive that ends without its two end blocks stops with 'archive
    ends early'. A file over 4 GB minus 1 byte is skipped on a FAT32 card
    ('too large for FAT32'); exFAT takes it.

MEMORY
    Reading: about 21 KB of heap while it runs, and 40 KB more for a
    gzipped archive (zlib, shown as 'zlib' in 'mem'); 8 bytes more per file
    written, to catch case clashes. Making: about 15 KB, and 56 KB more
    with z. It is refused when that would leave less than 64 KB free. One
    of tar, gunzip, zcat, gzip and fetch runs at a time across all shells;
    another is refused with 'busy: <command> is running in another shell'.

CREATING
    'tar cf <archive> <path>...' puts each path in, a directory with all
    it holds, in the order the card lists them; up to 16 directories deep
    (a deeper one is skipped and said). Paths are relative to -C <dir>, or
    to the working directory, and stored as given: 'tar cf a.tar -C /apps
    x' stores 'x/...'. A leading / is removed (said once); a path with
    '..' is refused, since 'tar xf' would refuse to extract it. The
    archive is ustar, with pax records for names over 100 characters, so
    any tar on a computer reads it: files have mode 0644, directories
    0755, owner 0, and the card's dates (UTC).

    Skipped, said and counted, while the rest goes in: the archive itself
    and its .part, a file being written (as 'ls' shows it, or open for
    writing in another shell or over SFTP), a file that can't be read, a
    path that doesn't exist, a name longer than 'tar xf' reads back.

    The archive is written to '<archive>.part' and renamed at the end; an
    existing archive is replaced only with -f. Ctrl-C, a write error or a
    full card stops it ('stopped: <why>; <archive> not made') and removes
    the .part; so does an archive with nothing in it ('nothing to
    archive'). At the end: '<archive>: <n> files, <n> directories, <size>
    bytes (<n> skipped)'.

SEE ALSO
    gunzip, gzip, fetch, sha256sum, ls, mkdir
@@ tee
NAME
    tee - write a command's output to a file and pass it on

SYNOPSIS
    <command> | tee [-a] <path>

DESCRIPTION
    Writes everything the command prints to the file, and passes it on
    unchanged, so the screen (or the next stage) still gets it: 'dmesg | tee
    /dmesg.txt' shows dmesg and saves it, and 'dmesg | tee /d.txt | more'
    pages it as well. The file is replaced; with -a the output is appended
    to it. The path is relative to the working directory, as for the file
    commands, and the same paths are protected as for 'echo': only the
    console may write under /certs and /var/log.

    The file is written as the output comes, and a command stopped with Ctrl-C
    leaves what it had written, as on Unix. Stages before tee decide what it
    sees: 'dmesg | grep wifi | tee /w.txt' saves only the matching lines.
    While a long command runs, the file is synced to the card about once a
    second (at the first output after each second mark), so 'ls' shows its
    size meanwhile and a reset loses at most the output since the last sync.
    If the card refuses a write (it is full, say) tee says so once and stops
    the command, as head does.

NOTES
    tee works only after a '|': typed on its own it says so. It takes one
    path; quote one with spaces ('tee "my notes.txt"'). A path spelled
    exactly '-a' is taken as the option: write './-a'.

    '<command> > <path>' is a quiet tee: the shell adds one as the last
    stage that writes the file and passes nothing on, so nothing reaches the
    screen ('dmesg | grep wifi > /w.txt'); '>>' appends. Its messages are
    tee's. The command's own messages (its usage, its errors) go into the
    file too; only an unknown command is said on the screen, and then no
    file is made. A '>' ends the line, and doesn't go with '| more'. More about
    pipes in 'man more'.

SEE ALSO
    echo, cat, more, head, grep
@@ temp
NAME
    temp - the chip's temperature, and the lowest and highest since boot

SYNOPSIS
    temp
    temp cal [<degrees> | off]          (change: console only)

DESCRIPTION
    The RP2350's own temperature sensor, read every 10 s, with the lowest and
    highest readings since boot and how long ago each was taken. It is logged
    with the supply voltage (see 'power'): at boot, when it has moved a degree
    or the supply 0.2 V (at most once a minute), when USB power comes or goes,
    and at least once an hour.

    It is the chip's temperature, not the room's: the chip runs warmer than
    the air, more so under Wi-Fi load. Uncalibrated, the sensor can be a few
    degrees off, but it is good for change over time on one board. The last
    line of 'temp' says whether a calibration is set. The glitch detector's
    report ('glitch') includes the last reading before a detector reset.

    'temp cal <degrees>' calibrates it: give what a thermometer beside the
    board reads now, in degrees C (e.g. 23.5; -40 to 125). The difference
    from the sensor's own reading, at most 15 degrees either way, is saved and
    added to every reading after: 'temp', the log and the glitch detector's
    reports. 'temp cal' alone shows it; 'temp cal off' removes it, and so does
    'config reset' ('temp_cal' in a provisioning file sets it).

    To calibrate: leave the board unpowered beside the thermometer for half an
    hour, power it up, and within a minute of Wi-Fi connecting run 'temp cal'
    with the thermometer's reading. The chip is already a little warmer than
    the room by then, so a calibrated reading is closer to the room's
    temperature as seen from the chip than to the chip's own: don't compare it
    with the chip's rated maximum. It corrects the offset only, so it is best
    near the temperature it was set at.

SEE ALSO
    power, glitch, config, dmesg
@@ time
NAME
    time - the clock, the RTC and time sync over NTS

SYNOPSIS
    time
    time sync
    time sync sntp                      (console only)
    time auto [<hours> | off]           (change: console only)
    time cal [<ppm> | off]              (change: console only)
    time server [<name> | default]      (change: console only)
    time zone [<name> | utc]            (change: console only)
    time zone list [<prefix>]
    time zone install

DESCRIPTION
    All times are UTC: the clock, the RTC, file times, logs. A time zone only
    adds a line of local time to 'time' (see TIME ZONES). The RTC (a PCF8523
    on the Adalogger, with its coin cell) is the source of truth: at boot the
    clock is set from it. Network time comes from NTS (Network Time Security,
    authenticated NTP): a TLS key exchange with the NTS server on port 4460,
    then NTP queries that only accept replies that check out under the
    server's key. There is no fallback to plain NTP: without NTS and without
    an RTC time, the clock stays unset.

    'time' shows the date and time and where it came from (RTC, NTS or SNTP),
    the RTC's reading and backup-battery state, the calibration, the drift
    the last sync measured, the last sync with its correction and round trip,
    a failed attempt and when it is retried, the server and the periodic
    setting.

    'time sync' syncs the clock and the RTC from NTS now. The board also syncs
    by itself when the RTC has no valid time (retrying with doubling delays),
    and periodically if 'time auto <hours>' (1 to 720; default off) is set:
    one interval after the last successful sync, or after boot if there has
    been none. A correction of more than 5 minutes is logged as a warning.
    'time sync sntp' does one plain, unauthenticated SNTP sync on request.

    'time server <name>' sets the NTS server (host name or IPv4 address);
    'default' goes back to the build-time one (time.cloudflare.com). Its
    certificate must chain to a trusted root ('tls roots').

    The 'drift:' line: every sync reads the RTC just before setting it, and
    compares it with the time it was set to at the sync before (kept in flash
    across restarts and power cuts). So after two syncs it shows the RTC's
    rate, e.g. 'rtc +92.0+-13 ppm in 22 h (no cal)': positive means fast, and
    the figure after +- is how far off the measurement may be (the RTC counts
    whole seconds, so the longer between the syncs, the better: about 15
    hours for 20 ppm). When the two syncs fell in one uptime it also shows the
    board's own crystal ('xosc'). The 'rtc-drift' health check judges both.

    'time cal <ppm>' corrects the RTC's rate: 'time sync', a day or more,
    'time sync' again, then give the rate 'drift:' shows, e.g. 'time cal 92'
    (the 'rtc-drift' check's warning suggests the value). The RTC is slowed
    or sped up by the nearest step of 4.34 ppm and 'time' shows the rate
    applied ('cal: slowed by 91.1 ppm'). The value replaces the previous one:
    with a calibration set, give the rate shown on the 'cal:' line plus the
    new drift. The next sync after a change starts a new measurement. 'time
    cal off' removes it. Saved, and written into the RTC at boot if the chip
    doesn't have it.

TIME ZONES
    'time zone <name>' sets the zone 'time' shows local time in, by its IANA
    name (Europe/Madrid, America/New_York; case doesn't matter, and a space
    may stand for '_'). The name is looked up in /tz/zones.bundle on the
    card, and the name and the zone's rule (a POSIX TZ string such as
    CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3) are saved, so local time shows with the card
    out too. A name that isn't found gets the closest ones, and on the
    console Tab completes the names from the card ('time zone Europe/Ma'
    and Tab). 'time zone utc' clears it. Then 'time' adds a line:

        local:  2026-11-27 14:03:05 CET (UTC+01:00, Europe/Madrid)

    'time zone' shows the zone, its rule, local time, and the tzdata release
    of the card's bundle; if the card's rule for the zone differs from the
    saved one, it says so, and 'time zone <name>' again takes the card's.

    'time zone list' lists the zones on the card with their rules, those
    starting with <prefix> if one is given; 'time zone list europe/ | more'
    pages them.

    'time zone install' downloads tz/zones.bundle from the main branch of
    the SOS repository on GitHub into /tz (the board needs the network),
    checks every line of it, and says whether the saved zone's rule changed.
    It works from any shell, SSH included, on purpose: the card can be
    written over SFTP anyway, and only 'time zone <name>' on the console
    changes the setting. From a computer, tools/tz-install.sh <host> copies
    it over SFTP. The bundle is built from IANA's signed tzdata releases.

    Only what is shown changes. Log files, file times (ls, SFTP), chat and
    wall stamps and log rotation stay UTC and say so.

SEE ALSO
    nts, tls, config
@@ tls
NAME
    tls - the root certificates TLS trusts

SYNOPSIS
    tls roots
    tls roots approve      (console only)
    tls roots forget       (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    TLS (used by NTS and the HTTPS client) trusts the built-in roots (SSL.com
    Root Certification Authority ECC, for Cloudflare's time server; ISRG Root
    X1, Let's Encrypt) and, optionally, extra ones from the card in
    /certs/roots.pem: a PEM bundle (text between the certificates is fine) or
    DER certificates, up to 64 KB and 32 certificates. The file is used only
    while it is exactly the file approved on the console:

      1. Upload it anywhere with SFTP, e.g. 'put roots.pem incoming/roots.pem'.
      2. On the console: 'mkdir /certs' (once), then
         'mv /incoming/roots.pem /certs/roots.pem'.
      3. On the console: 'tls roots approve'. It lists each certificate
         (common name, key type, whether it is a CA, expiry, SHA-256
         fingerprint), checks TLS can use it, and after 'yes' trusts exactly
         that file; its SHA-256 is saved with the configuration.

    Only the console can change anything under /certs, under any spelling.
    A changed file isn't used until approved again: TLS then logs a warning
    once and uses the built-in roots alone, as it does when the file or the
    card is missing. A private key, or any PEM block other than a certificate,
    makes the file invalid. 'tls roots' shows the built-in roots and the
    file's state; 'tls roots forget' stops trusting it.

    While the clock isn't set, certificate dates can't be checked: the first
    key exchange accepts certificates whatever their dates (signatures and the
    host name are still checked) and logs it.

SEE ALSO
    time, http, config
@@ uptime
NAME
    uptime - time since boot, and what the last reset was

SYNOPSIS
    uptime
    uptime history

DESCRIPTION
    The time since the board started, and why it last reset:

        (last reset: power-on)          power applied
        (last reset: reboot)            asked for: 'reboot', picotool, flashing
        (last reset: reset pin)         the RUN pin
        (last reset: brownout)          the core supply dipped under the
                                        brownout detector's threshold
        (last reset: watchdog timeout)  the main loop stopped coming round
        (last reset: glitch detector)   the detector reset the chip ('glitch')
        (last reset: debugger)          a reset asked through SWD
        last reset: hard fault ...      a fault or panic, with the addresses
                                        to look up in build/sos.dis

    'uptime history' lists the boots kept in flash, newest first: when (if
    the clock was set), why, and how long the board had been up before it ('?'
    after a power-on, a brownout or the reset pin, which leave nothing to read
    it from), with the times a sync set the RTC between them. Up to 240
    records; when full, the newest 16 boots are kept. The 'resets' health
    check warns when three resets in a row, none asked for, each ended a run
    of under 3 minutes.

    Once flash has been written since boot (a configuration save, a console
    login), a 'flash writes:' line says how many times, and how long the last
    and the longest write held interrupts off, since nothing else runs during
    one.

SEE ALSO
    version, glitch, reboot, crash, health
@@ version
NAME
    version - firmware commit, board serial, chip revision, how it is secured

SYNOPSIS
    version

DESCRIPTION
    The first line names the commit the firmware was built from: 12 hex
    digits, '-dirty' if the build had uncommitted changes, 'unknown' if it was
    not built from a git checkout. It names the SOS commit only, not libraries
    a build takes from outside the tree. The same line greets the console
    above its first prompt and each SSH login.

    Then:

        board: serial ...     the serial number the board reports over USB
                              and to picotool
        chip: RP2350 A4 (revision 3, ROM 4)
                              the chip revision, from its revision field and
                              boot ROM version ('unknown revision' if the pair
                              isn't one this firmware knows)
        secure boot: on|off   as the chip latched it at reset
        debug port: enabled | Secure debug disabled | disabled
        root secret: in OTP | in flash (open board) | none
        console-only setup: no|yes
                              whether the board takes its settings only from
                              'setup' on its console, refusing a configuration
                              or host key written to its flash over USB (a
                              marker of its own in OTP, not secure boot; 'yes'
                              also when the marker can't be read)

    A further line appears only if the OTP rows behind the flags can't be read
    or say something else.

SEE ALSO
    bootkey, rootsecret, uptime
@@ wall
NAME
    wall - send a message to every other shell

SYNOPSIS
    wall <message>

DESCRIPTION
    Shows the message in every other shell at once, as

        [wall 14:02 UTC alice@laptop] message

    and says how many shells it was sent to. Times are UTC and say so, or time
    since boot ('+1h02m') until the clock is set. A message arrives between the
    reader's keystrokes: their line is cleared, the message printed, and the
    prompt and their typing redrawn; during a running command it appears on its
    own line. A full-screen program (edit, more, man) holds messages until it
    quits.

    Messages are kept only in memory (the last 16), never in the log or on the
    card. A reader whose client has stopped reading doesn't hold the sender up
    for more than 2 seconds; after that it misses messages and is told how many.

    Quote a message that holds an apostrophe, a '|' or '>' or repeated spaces:
    wall "don't reboot" (QUOTING in 'man intro').

SEE ALSO
    chat, who
@@ wc
NAME
    wc - count the lines, words and bytes of a command's output

SYNOPSIS
    <command> | wc

DESCRIPTION
    Prints, once the command has finished, three numbers: the newlines, the
    words and the bytes of its output ('help | wc', 'ls | wc'), counted as
    Unix wc counts them: a word is a run of anything but spaces, tabs and
    line breaks, and bytes are bytes, not characters. Ctrl-C while the command
    runs drops the count.

NOTES
    wc works only after a '|': typed on its own it says so. More about pipes
    in 'man more'.

SEE ALSO
    grep, head, tail, more
@@ who
NAME
    who - who is logged in, and who is in the chat

SYNOPSIS
    who

DESCRIPTION
    One line per shell: its slot (0 is the console), the person's name, where
    they are connected from, 'chat' if they are in the chat room, and how long
    they have been logged in. Yours is marked '*'.

    A person is named after the comment of the trusted key they logged in
    with ('alice@laptop'; 'key <n>' if it has none), the console is 'console',
    and a second login with the same key gets its slot added ('alice@laptop/2').

SEE ALSO
    wall, chat, ssh
@@ wifi
NAME
    wifi - Wi-Fi networks, state and radio settings

SYNOPSIS
    wifi
    wifi set | add | remove <n> | prefer <n> | connect     (console only)
    wifi country <XX> | worldwide                          (console only)
    wifi power [on | off]                            (change: console only)
    wifi stats [chip]
    wifi iovar <name> [<value>]                            (console only)
    wifi keepalive on | off                                (console only)

DESCRIPTION
    'wifi' lists the saved networks, numbered in the order they are preferred,
    each with whether a password is set and the connected one marked, then
    the connection state, power saving and country.

    'wifi set' changes the first network: it asks for the SSID (Tab offers the
    networks around) and the password (not echoed), saves them and reconnects.
    'wifi add' saves another (up to 5 in all), after the others. The board
    joins the first saved network in reach when it connects; while connected
    it doesn't notice a preferred network coming into reach until the link
    drops or 'wifi connect'. 'wifi remove <n>' forgets network n and
    reconnects if it was in use; 'wifi prefer <n>' moves it to the front, for
    the next connect. 'wifi connect' reconnects now, trying the saved networks
    from the first again.

    Wi-Fi connects in the background, retries after failures and reconnects
    if the link drops; the console is usable throughout. On a network with
    several access points the board ends up on the strongest; the log names
    it.

    'wifi country <XX>' sets the country whose radio rules to follow, as two
    capital letters (US), or 'worldwide' for the chip's worldwide set (the
    default). Saved; takes effect at the next boot.

    'wifi power on|off' turns power saving on or off (saved; default off).
    With it on the radio sleeps between beacons when idle: lower power, but
    the first packet after a quiet spell can take a little longer.

    'wifi stats' is for chasing packet loss: signal, noise floor, channel,
    access point and transmit rate; packets sent, received and dropped per
    protocol since boot; TCP segments resent; and how full lwIP's pools got.
    'wifi stats chip' adds the Wi-Fi chip's own counters, raw (index: value);
    their layout isn't published, so compare two readings.

    'wifi iovar' reads or sets a 32-bit variable in the Wi-Fi chip (2g_rate,
    ampdu_tx, qtxpower ...), for debugging; not saved, and an unknown name
    reads back as a meaningless value, not an error. 'wifi keepalive off'
    stops the once-a-minute address announcement until reboot, for testing
    (see 'net').

SEE ALSO
    net, setup, hostname, config
@@ zcat
NAME
    zcat - print a gzip file decompressed

SYNOPSIS
    zcat <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the file decompressed, as 'cat' prints a file, and leaves it as
    it is: 'zcat /var/log/old.log.gz | grep error'. Any name is accepted.
    The decoding is gunzip's: joined gzip files print one after the other,
    and a corrupt file prints what came before the damage, then the error.
    Zero bytes after the end are skipped without a note, so that the output
    is only the data.

    A job: Ctrl-C stops it, and a slow SSH client slows it down instead of
    losing output. It takes the same memory as gunzip, and one of the two
    runs at a time across all shells ('man gunzip').

SEE ALSO
    gunzip, cat, more
